ience this is what you're  gonna do  the lecturer actually um had the best  example of spinning something I've ever  seen in my life when he was talking  about this project called the Mars  atmosphere orbiter and how he was  working on this project which  unfortunately crashed into the surface  of Mars because of a miscommunication  between two science teams and he spawned  this really embarrassing story by saying  that as a result I did work on the  project which got the first bit of  British equipment on Martian soil I mean  it wasn't one piece but you know on the  social side of thing  on Monday I went to a 21st birthday  party which was being hosted at a  cocktail bar in Oxford before we went  out to a club whilst I also went to a  yoga class on Thursday which is  something that's put on by the college  welfare officer so one of the elected  positions in the JCR is a welfare  officer who's in charge of various  things which are they designed to boost  morale or be somebody to talk to if  you're having a tough time I've never  personally had to take advantage of the  more serious side at service but yoga  class is the kind of thing that is nice  to do in your downtime and it's quite  relaxing and it actually means you're  going to work better in the future  because you're not stressed about  everything so my last video I asked you  guys where an Oxford you'd like me to  take my camera next and judging by a  couple of responses I got the impression  you wanted me to look at the physics  department so physics in Oxford is split  between three sites you've got the  atmospheric physics department and the  Clarendon laboratory on Parkes road and  the Dennis Wilkinson building on  keyboard now starting with the  atmospheric physics department  technically it is the atmospheric  Oceanic and planetary physics department  and it has what you'd expect a  department to have really it's got  corridors offices and elect theatres  where I have my atmospheric fourth-year  option Fletcher's the Clandon lab is  where amongst other things Henry Moseley  provided the experimental justification  for the atomic number these days at  houses research in atomic physics  ultracold atoms biophysics and a couple  of other small research groups it also  has the cafe that students can use and a  big lecture theatre where you have all  your lectures in first and third year  and some fourth your options and another  smaller one which is where you have your  lectures in second year and you may also  recognize as being the setting for  physics authority lectures the Dennis  Workington building which is named after  Sir Dennis Wilkinson he was a notable  nuclear physicist Oxford contains  research these days for nuclear or  particle physics and astrophysics it  also has a cafe and a lecture theatre  which is where I have my full theer  theoretical physics options lectures in  and houses the undergraduate teaching  labs now the teaching labs are split by  subject so there are labs for  again amongst other things computing or  programming in other words optics which  is kind of necessarily dark and  electronics and that's pretty much it  for the physics department short of  physically sticking my camera into  people's  that's about as much as I can show you  but if you'd like more information about  the research which happens and I highly  recommend you check out the Oxford  website which I again put in the  doobly-doo last but not least I have a  question for you the audience have you  decided on the college that you'd like  to apply to yet if so why and if not  what are you looking for in a Knox  bridge College answers that a nots and  Peter's College will be tolerated but  with a heavy judgment and my question to  you jamie is who in your time Oxford has  been your favorite lecturer and why  looking forward to your video on  Thursday and good luck to everybody  who's involved in the Ox tweet project
 

